-- Passage rg11-character-setting-plot-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('bcd597d4-e261-5b65-9da7-4aad26879c5e', 'rg11-character-setting-plot-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Last Gather$q$, $q$The bowl sat on the cooling shelf with a crack running from its rim to its center, thin as a hair and impossible to miss. Ines Achterberg stood in front of it at seven in the morning, still wearing yesterday's sweatshirt, and tried to remember the exact second she had ruined it.

Yesterday afternoon the studio had been loud with the roar of the furnace and the clink of steel pipes against their racks. Mr. Haddad, who had run the glass shop behind the community arts center for thirty years, had given her the final piece of her apprenticeship: a wide amber bowl, the kind he sold at the winter market. He watched without speaking while she gathered the molten glass, turned the pipe, and shaped the rim with wet newspaper folded in her palm. Everything had gone well until the very end, when she noticed a small bubble near the base and pushed the bowl back into the furnace to reheat it one more time. She knew the rule about overheating thin glass. She did it anyway, because she wanted the bowl to be perfect.

Mr. Haddad had said only one thing as she set it in the cooling oven: "Glass remembers everything you do to it."

Now he came through the side door carrying two cups of tea and set one beside her. He looked at the crack for a long moment, and then he did something she did not expect. He picked up the bowl, carried it to the front window, and placed it on the display shelf where customers would see it first.

"That is not for sale," Ines said.

"No," he agreed. "It is for you. Every morning you will walk past the bowl that taught you when to stop."

Ines wanted to argue. Instead she picked up her pipe, checked the furnace temperature, and began a second gather.$q$, 'draft')
