   $q$What is the main effect of opening the story with the cracked bowl rather than with the making of the bowl?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Readers are surprised at the end when they learn the bowl was damaged in the furnace.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Readers are led to suspect that Mr. Haddad cracked the bowl while Ines was away.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Readers learn the steps of glassblowing in order before any conflict is introduced.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-sequence"},{"text":"Readers know the outcome at once, so interest shifts from whether Ines fails to why she does.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Readers know the outcome at once, so interest shifts from whether Ines fails to why she does.$q$, $q$Because the crack is shown first, the flashback works as an explanation of how and why it happened. There is no surprise ending about the damage, since the very first sentence reveals it.$q$, 'draft', 'bcd597d4-e261-5b65-9da7-4aad26879c5e')
