-- Passage rg11-character-setting-plot-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1a9e65b8-39e1-50b6-902a-f3c8c4deb422', 'rg11-character-setting-plot-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Line Seven$q$, $q$In the winter of 1923, the town of Marrow Creek had exactly one telephone switchboard, and it sat in the back room of the dry goods store where Hattie Lindqvist worked the night shift. The room was barely wider than her chair. A coal stove ticked in one corner, a calendar from the feed mill hung on the wall, and in front of her rose the board itself: rows of brass jacks, each one a house or a farm or a shop she had never been inside.

Hattie had lived in Marrow Creek only four months. She knew almost no one by face. She knew nearly everyone by voice.

At half past ten, the lamp for Line Seven lit up. That was the Castellanos farm, three miles out on the river road. Hattie plugged in her cord.

"Number, please."

A girl's voice, thin and careful, asked for Dr. Pruitt. Her mother had a high fever, she said, and her father was away hauling lumber until Friday. Hattie rang the doctor's house. No answer. She rang again. Then she remembered that on Tuesdays Dr. Pruitt played checkers at the hotel, because she had connected his calls there for weeks without ever meeting him.

She rang the hotel. Within a minute the doctor was on the line, and within ten he was in his car on the river road.

Hattie stayed connected to Line Seven, talking quietly with the girl, whose name was Lucia, about the snow and the stove and which chores could wait until morning. Near midnight, Lucia said the headlights had arrived, and the line went quiet.

Hattie pulled the cord and leaned back. The stove ticked. The board was dark except for one small lamp, a late call from the depot. She had still not seen a single face that night, but she felt, for the first time, that she lived somewhere.$q$, 'draft')
