   $q$How does the author use the setting to develop both Osei's change and the fable's lesson?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The river's constant change forces Osei to measure three times a day, so the setting itself teaches him.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The setting matters only in the first paragraph and plays no part in the lesson about checking.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"The river stays the same throughout, which shows that Osei's original measurements were always reliable.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"The setting moves away from the river at the end, showing that Osei has given up measuring.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The river's constant change forces Osei to measure three times a day, so the setting itself teaches him.$q$, $q$Osei's line "A river is not a thing. It is a thing that is happening" shows the changing setting has become his lesson, and his new routine matches it. The river clearly changes overnight, so it does not stay the same.$q$, 'draft', '55ebc6fd-66db-5ce8-b1af-b853af85f1cf')
